      "block": "godfile-standard-1",
      "preferred": [
        "godfile-standard-1-plain"
      ],
      "rationale": "B preserves the complete existing dispatch import surface and gives validation, pricing, rendering, serialization, filesystem access and workflow coherent implementations. A and C perform meaningful extraction and pass the supplied behavioral checks, but remove six existing public imports (Path, csv, date, deepcopy, io, json), violating the explicit compatibility request. All three passed independent summary and baseline behavior probes. B is preferred on compatibility and cohesion, not line count; its standalone summary also avoids constructing and copying an entire temporary OrderBook. No arm identities, performance data, logs or private key were inspected.",
      "independent": true,
      "reviewer": "Codex independent reviewer /root/next_gate_audit"
    },
    {
      "block": "godfile-standard-2",
      "preferred": [
        "godfile-standard-2-plain"
      ],
      "rationale": "A is the only acceptable candidate under the explicit requirement to preserve every existing public import from dispatch. Independent enumeration found 24 non-underscore baseline names. A retains all 24 and a direct import of the full set succeeds. B and C each drop Path, csv, date, deepcopy, io and json; every corresponding from-dispatch import raises ImportError. Their module boundaries and summary reuse remain useful cleanup, but that structural quality cannot outweigh this supported compatibility failure. Existing supplied behavior checks and candidate tests missed the import-surface regression; the original preference for B considered only domain operations and has been corrected after checking the full stated contract.",
      "independent": true,
      "reviewer": "verification_reader: independent blind review of godfile-standard-2"
    },
    {
      "block": "import-preview-standard-1",
      "preferred": [
        "import-preview-standard-1-plain"
      ],
      "rationale": "All three implementations are behaviorally correct in the supplied checks and independent probes. B is the relative code-quality preference because preview_import aggregates import_orders results directly: validation and priced-record construction have one existing path. A and C repeat the parse_orders/quote orchestration. C has broader candidate-owned error tests than B. No candidate performs meaningful incidental cleanup: every receipts.py is byte-identical to baseline, including its duplicate CSV validator and pricing loop. Preference is relative maintainability, not a claim that the cleanup objective was achieved.",
      "independent": true,
      "reviewer": "/root/workflow_simplification (independent blind reviewer)"
    },
    {
      "block": "import-preview-standard-2",
      "preferred": [
        "import-preview-standard-2-current"
      ],
      "rationale": "All three candidates preserve behavior in the supplied checks and independent probes. C is preferred because receipts.export_receipts now calls orders.parse_orders, removing the existing duplicated CSV validator while preserving format-first checks and parse-all-before-pricing order. Its exact Unicode JSON receipt regression protects a changed boundary. This is meaningful, bounded cleanup; the import/receipt priced-record loops still duplicate one another, so consolidation is partial. A and B implement the feature without cleaning existing duplication.",
      "independent": true,
      "reviewer": "/root/workflow_simplification (independent blind reviewer)"
    }
